# Independent docs-only HTTP smoke review

Read tools/site/smoke.py and test_smoke.py on feat/doc-pages-smoke against spec15's direct route, fixed checked artifact, bounded observation and no false publication/LKG rules. No source edits. Parent owns implementation corrections.

Open concrete finding: all requests append ?nepl3-smoke=<manifest digest>. Consequently the ordinary deployed URL and its normal cache key are never observed, although the success receipt names the query-free URL. A real loopback reproduction served exact checked bytes for query-bearing URLs but returned503 for ordinary /NEPL3/. smoke.run reported passed, and a separate direct GET returned503. Evidence: query-only-repro.json in this directory. Keep ordinary path checks for document/assets/directory indexes/missing route; use no-cache request headers and, if desired, additional distinctly recorded cache-busted identity probes. Do not let a cache-busted response alone stand in for the direct-access/reload requirement. Add a query-only-success negative fixture and record request URLs or query mode accurately.

Independently ran python -m unittest discover -s tools/site -p test_*.py -v:11 passed (7 payload plus4 smoke test methods). Real-server subcases cover changed/extra-byte/missing payload, wrong CSS MIME, SPA200 fallback, redirect rejection and outer deadline. Existing normal-case tests assert every request has the digest query, so they currently codify the uncovered behavior rather than testing canonical URLs.

Confirmed useful behavior: HTTPS default with certificate/hostname verification; explicit IPv4 loopback HTTP+port mode; no proxy or cookie handler; redirect_request refusal and explicit status check; endpoint/base/query/fragment/credential constraints; manifest+all source bytes frozen via reviewed payload.snapshot; docs-only and40hex input commit checks; build.json checked before/after file loop; all declared payload except explicitly omitted .nojekyll fetched, with manifest itself included; directory/index aliases;404 probe; HTML/CSS MIME and identity Content-Encoding; expected length+1 body bound; outer subprocess wall-clock timeout<=300seconds kills a slow worker independent of socket inactivity timeout. The checker does not infer Pages deployment identity, whole-CDN atomic update or LKG; publication_verified false is correct even on HTTPS success.

Receipts include actual observed digest/byte count/status/MIME on success, selected input commit/manifest and transport mode. On a failure, the worker returns bounded exception detail and no passed result; the outer CLI exits1 for failed observations. Partial successful rows are discarded on error. A future publisher must retain invocation/deployment journal alongside failure output; this module is not already an incident/LKG state machine. A failure to serialize/launch the worker is an error rather than a fabricated passed report.

No implementation change is needed merely because publisher/LKG, interactive/browser execution, JS/CSP keyboard or remote deployment control-plane proof are not present: those remain explicitly outside this transfer/HTTP stage. The current concrete blocker is direct URL coverage. Scope approval awaits that correction and focused re-review; the eleven existing tests do not establish it.

## Re-review after canonical URL correction

Read final smoke/test implementation: get() defaults to no query; only first and last build.json identity checks pass cache_bust=True. The regular file loop includes a separate canonical build.json request. Every observation now records its actual URL. No-cache headers remain; redirect/status/body/MIME/deadline safeguards are unchanged. Root also includes URL/manifest/transport context in failed worker results.

Reused the saved independent fixture and exact query-only server behavior. Formerly passed; now returns failed with ValueError non-200 response for build.json after one cache-busted200 and the canonical503. query-only-fixed.json records both actual requests and failure. This closes the concrete false-success finding without changing production in the review.

Independently reran all11 payload/smoke unittest methods after correction: passed. The new query-only negative subcase is present. Reviewed the normal-case query count and explicit direct directory request checks. Independently reran the reviewed local HTTP runner against actual .tmp/site-final-primary, preserving separate output real-site-fixed.json here: passed with26 observations. Only the two outer build identity requests have queries; all canonical payload/dirindex/404 requests are direct. Root's parallel result .tmp/site-smoke-result.json was also inspected, not mistaken for this independent run.

No remaining finding for the bounded docs-only HTTP byte checker. Approval is limited to these code/loopback observations; HTTPS production, Pages deployment identity, whole-CDN/cache convergence, browser interaction and LKG remain explicitly unverified. All success receipts continue publication_verified=false. The previous initial finding above is retained as review history and is now closed.
